const body = `<h1>Unit 7: ArrayList</h1>

<p>
    Arrays from Unit 6 are <strong>fixed-size</strong>. You decide the size when you create
    them, and that's it. But most collections in real programs grow and shrink. Your inventory
    shouldn't have a cap. Your spellbook shouldn't be locked at 8 slots. Your leaderboard
    shouldn't need a pre-determined size.
</p>

<p>
    This unit introduces <strong>ArrayList</strong> — a dynamic, resizable list that grows
    as you add things. You'll also learn sorting (selection sort, insertion sort) to organize
    your collections, and the standard search pattern for finding items.
</p>

<h2>Concepts</h2>

<table>
    <tr><th>Topic</th><th>What you'll learn</th></tr>
    <tr>
        <td><a href="arraylist-basics.html">ArrayList Basics</a></td>
        <td>Declaration, add, remove, get, set, size. Generic types.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><a href="arraylist-traversal.html">Traversal &amp; Algorithms</a></td>
        <td>For-each loop. Finding, counting, filtering, accumulating with ArrayList.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><a href="sorting.html">Sorting</a></td>
        <td>Selection sort and insertion sort. Comparing elements.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><a href="reading-files.html">Reading Files: File + Scanner</a></td>
        <td>hasNext/nextLine loop, all JQR methods, the exception story, loaders. New CED 4.6.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><a href="searching-sorting.html">Searching &amp; Sorting</a></td>
        <td>Linear and binary search, the sorted precondition, trace tables, sort trade-offs. CED 4.14/4.15.</td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2>Teacher Projects (Standalone Demos)</h2>

<table>
    <tr><th>Project</th><th>What it demonstrates</th></tr>
    <tr>
        <td><a href="leaderboard.html">Leaderboard</a></td>
        <td>ArrayList of highscores. Add, sort, display top-N. Dynamic sizing.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><a href="music-playlist.html">Music Playlist</a></td>
        <td>ArrayList of songs. Add, remove, shuffle, search. Full CRUD operations.</td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2>Your RPG Project</h2>

<table>
    <tr><th>Guide</th><th>What you'll build</th></tr>
    <tr>
        <td><a href="dynamic-inventory.html">DynamicInventory.java (Student RPG Guide)</a></td>
        <td>Replace your fixed inventory array with an ArrayList. Add items, remove items, search by name, sort by value. No more "inventory full" messages.</td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2>RPG Thread</h2>

<p>
    Your inventory from Unit 6 gets upgraded from Item[] to ArrayList&lt;Item&gt;. The sorting
    algorithms you learn here (selection sort, insertion sort) are the same ones that sort
    your inventory by value or your leaderboard by score. In Unit 8, 2D arrays work alongside
    ArrayList — the dungeon map is a grid, but your party roster is a dynamic list.
</p>

<h2>Prerequisites</h2>

<p>Before starting this unit, you should be comfortable with:</p>
<ul>
    <li>Arrays, traversal, and array algorithms: <a href="../unit-06/index.html">Unit 6</a></li>
    <li>Writing classes, constructors, and methods: <a href="../unit-05/index.html">Unit 5</a></li>
    <li>Loops (while, for, for-each): <a href="../unit-04/index.html">Unit 4</a></li>
</ul>

<h2>Reference Materials</h2>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Quick Reference:</strong> <a href="../reference/unit-07-quickref.html">ArrayList Quick Ref</a> &mdash; methods, traversal, searching and sorting, file loading</li>
</ul>

<h2>Assessment</h2>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Part A:</strong> Knowledge — ArrayList vs array, generic types, ArrayList methods</li>
    <li><strong>Part B:</strong> Code trace — predict output of ArrayList operations and traversal</li>
    <li><strong>Part C:</strong> Write code — implement an ArrayList-based solution from a specification</li>
</ul>`;
